# Patent Claim

## Claim 1

A recombinant microbial expression system for the biocatalytic conversion of flavone glycosides to dihydrochalcone derivatives, comprising:

(a) a recombinant host cell transformed with:

&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(i) a first nucleic acid sequence encoding a chalcone isomerase-reductase enzyme having at least 90% sequence identity to SEQ ID NO: 1, said nucleic acid sequence having at least 85% sequence identity to SEQ ID NO: 2, and encoding a polypeptide capable of catalyzing the reduction of the C2-C3 double bond of the flavone C-ring;

&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(ii) a second nucleic acid sequence encoding an NADPH-dependent enone reductase having at least 90% sequence identity to SEQ ID NO: 3, said nucleic acid sequence having at least 85% sequence identity to SEQ ID NO: 4;

&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(iii) optionally, a third nucleic acid sequence encoding a glycosyltransferase enzyme having at least 85% sequence identity to SEQ ID NO: 5, said nucleic acid sequence having at least 80% sequence identity to SEQ ID NO: 6, wherein said glycosyltransferase catalyzes regioselective glycosylation of the dihydrochalcone product at the 4'-O position;

(b) wherein said first and second nucleic acid sequences are operably linked to heterologous promoter sequences functional in said host cell, said promoters selected from the group consisting of T7, tac, and constitutive fungal promoters;

(c) wherein said host cell is selected from the group consisting of *Escherichia coli*, *Saccharomyces cerevisiae*, and *Pichia pastoris*;

(d) wherein contacting said recombinant host cell, or a cell lysate or purified enzyme preparation derived therefrom, with a flavone glycoside substrate selected from the group consisting of naringin, neohesperidin, and hesperidin, in the presence of a reduced nicotinamide cofactor selected from NADPH or NADH, and under conditions permitting enzymatic activity, produces a corresponding dihydrochalcone glycoside product selected from the group consisting of phloridzin, trilobatin, and neohesperidin dihydrochalcone, with a conversion yield of at least 70% as determined by HPLC analysis.

## Claim 2 (Dependent)

The recombinant microbial expression system of Claim 1, wherein said chalcone isomerase-reductase enzyme comprises the amino acid sequence of SEQ ID NO: 1 with conservative substitutions at positions corresponding to residues 45, 112, and 187, wherein said substitutions do not abolish enzymatic activity.

## Claim 3 (Dependent)

The recombinant microbial expression system of Claim 1, wherein said host cell further comprises a fourth nucleic acid sequence encoding a glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase for regeneration of said reduced nicotinamide cofactor, said fourth nucleic acid sequence having at least 90% sequence identity to SEQ ID NO: 7.

## Claim 4 (Method Claim)

A method for producing a dihydrochalcone glycoside comprising:
(a) culturing the recombinant host cell of Claim 1 under conditions sufficient for expression of said first and second polypeptides;
(b) contacting said cultured host cell, or lysate thereof, with a flavone glycoside substrate in a reaction medium comprising a reduced nicotinamide cofactor;
(c) incubating said reaction medium at a temperature between 25°C and 37°C for a period sufficient to achieve conversion of said flavone glycoside substrate to said dihydrochalcone glycoside product; and
(d) isolating said dihydrochalcone glycoside product from said reaction medium.

---

*Note: SEQ ID NO placeholders (1–7) are illustrative identifiers referencing a formal Sequence Listing to be appended per 37 C.F.R. § 1.821–1.825 (or equivalent PCT/EPO requirements) in an actual filing, wherein the corresponding nucleotide and amino acid sequences would be explicitly disclosed.*
